WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.330
Film Courage: I’ve heard you say something
about ‘the big secret that never gets told

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.620
to anyone outside of Hollywood is that most
films are greenlit before the final script

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.620
is made.’

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.010
So do tell.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.840
Because for a lot of people who aren’t from
here or aren’t familiar with the business,

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.890
I think they’d be sort of shocked to know
this?

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:27.710
Scott Kirkpatrick: It’s important to remember
that movies and TV shows cost an incredible

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.500
amount of money to produce.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:37.620
And if there is nobody to watch it on the
backside or to transact upon it, on iTunes

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.589
or at a movie theater, then there is not much
of a point in doing that.

00:00:43.589 --> 00:00:48.789
And there is an even more expensive aspect
to producing something which is marketing

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:51.989
it and getting it out in front of people so
they know it exists.

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:57.710
So that when they’re interested in seeing
it and ready to buy tickets or download it

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:01.889
behind a paywall and pay money for it, they
have the ability to do it and they’re ready

00:01:01.889 --> 00:01:04.019
to do it right away.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.979
That’s the financial end.

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:16.180
People in my position…from where I am working
all the way up to top executives at studios,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.960
what those people (the end-users) want to
see is really, really important.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.680
And that is kind of where we’re thinking
because if the end-users are interested in

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:32.289
seeing it, then international countries would
be interested in also having access to it.

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:36.649
And they would be willing to invest money
to have access to it early which is where

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:40.399
the whole pre-sale games comes into play.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:46.979
Or a TV channel here in the US, if they know
their audience want something very specific

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:54.079
they will work to have that produced on their
network and they will kind of work backwards,

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:57.939
first finding out what kind of project its
got to be, the types of people who have to

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:03.909
be in it, and then working their way backwards
to get to the point of actually drafting writing

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:05.929
and creating and all that.

00:02:05.929 --> 00:02:11.580
On the big scale for these major, major productions
at the studio level of a major network level,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:17.840
that’s why they go for things like major
franchises or very well-known books or very

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.660
well known media properties are doing re-makes.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.860
There is already an audience there.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:27.680
They kind of have an estimate of how it’s
going to get from Point A to Point B and that

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.920
is where the business side of it really plays.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.630
It’s what is it, how is it going to work,
where is the money coming from, and who is

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.190
going to get paid what to make this whole
process happen?

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:43.010
That’s got to all be figured out before
you really hone in on the right script.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:50.250
Most screenwriting books approach it as: you
write a story and it evolves into a movie.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:55.360
And this whole industry doesn’t exist with
people twiddling their thumbs, waiting for

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.930
the right script to drop into their hands.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.760
It works in the opposite direction which is
we know we need this type of content to be

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.260
successful.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:09.770
We have certain channels or certain platforms
that we have to cater with content so that’s

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:13.700
the stuff we’re going to be looking for
as finished content or we’ll go make it

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:14.700
ourselves.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.020
That’s what MarVista did really well.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:22.180
In [Scott’s book] Writing for the Greenlight,
the whole first chapter is exactly about that

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:23.180
idea.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.070
I used to go to the Cannes Film Festival,
we used to go to Berlin (to the major film

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.070
festivals).

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.070
I’d meet with my buyers.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.450
We’d have a slate of movies that we were
going to pitch and we would see which ones

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.500
had traction, which ones didn’t.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.190
We had a reputation for being able to produce
or deliver movies that would get from Point

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:42.310
A to Point B and be delivered on time and
be quality productions.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:47.510
We’d get the pre-sales first and then we’d
go back and say “Okay, write a movie based

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.510
on the pre-sales.”

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.730
Write a movie based on the poster we invented
to sell the pre-sales.”

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.320
That’s how it really works.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:55.460
Content is developed from the inside out.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:04:02.590
The occasional one-off to that or the occasional
exclusion is when major A-list talent who

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:07.510
already have a very well-established career
come up with unique ideas, but frankly they

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:14.791
can walk into CAA or a top executives office
in town (whatever) and say “Here’s an

00:04:14.791 --> 00:04:18.300
idea I’m thinking of” and it can be greenlit
right on the spot in the sense that they can

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:18.470
movie forward with it.

